{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mkdir -p verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/runs/run-20260426T190530Z--0zxve2uvg2/semantic/comment_section_generation && cat > verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/runs/run-20260426T190530Z--0zxve2uvg2/semantic/comment_section_generation/source--source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \\\"page_ref\\\": \\\"source:source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3\\\",\n  \\\"requested_count\\\": 5,\n  \\\"comments\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-1\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-maya-santoro\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"I like how the argument does not just pick at one axiom of strict orderhood. Treating truthmaking and alethic-fact grounding as species of grounding gives the paper a clear route from local counterexamples to a broader claim about grounding itself.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 22,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Accurately identifies the paper's species strategy and connects it to the strict-order thesis.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-2\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-eli-okafor\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"That species move is doing a lot of work, though. If someone thinks truthmaking is only related to grounding rather than a genuine case of it, the examples may show something important without settling the formal status of grounding in general.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": \\\"draft-1\\\",\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 20,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Raises a relevant limitation while staying anchored in the source's key premise.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-3\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-lina-kovac\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"The Socrates example is a neat pressure point on transitivity: Socrates being white can make the proposition about his whiteness true, and that proposition can make it true that there are propositions, but the first fact does not seem to make the existential claim true. That gap feels more intuitive than the set case the paper rejects.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 24,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Clearly reconstructs the non-transitivity example and contrasts it with the rejected Schaffer case.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-4\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-rory-chen\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"I am less sure about the self-grounding cases. A proposition that makes true its own existence claim or a truth fact that grounds itself may be formally possible, but the metaphysical cost is high enough that opponents might reinterpret the relation instead of giving up irreflexivity.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 18,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Engages the irreflexivity challenge thoughtfully, though it gestures at objections without developing them fully.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-5\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-samira-haddad\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"The reply about fundamentality seems important. If grounding can appear in bottomless or dense chains, then grounding need not always mark a step from less fundamental to more fundamental, which weakens the quick argument from explanation to asymmetry.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 23,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Faithfully captures the fundamentality objection and the source's response about bottomless or dense chains.\\\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\nJSON\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"mkdir -p verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/runs/run-20260426T190530Z--0zxve2uvg2/semantic/comment_section_generation && cat > verification/example_site/spaces/metaphysics/runs/run-20260426T190530Z--0zxve2uvg2/semantic/comment_section_generation/source--source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3.json <<'JSON'\n{\n  \\\"page_ref\\\": \\\"source:source-02-grounding-is-not-a-strict-order--7c76f525b4e3\\\",\n  \\\"requested_count\\\": 5,\n  \\\"comments\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-1\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-maya-santoro\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"I like how the argument does not just pick at one axiom of strict orderhood. Treating truthmaking and alethic-fact grounding as species of grounding gives the paper a clear route from local counterexamples to a broader claim about grounding itself.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 22,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Accurately identifies the paper's species strategy and connects it to the strict-order thesis.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-2\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-eli-okafor\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"That species move is doing a lot of work, though. If someone thinks truthmaking is only related to grounding rather than a genuine case of it, the examples may show something important without settling the formal status of grounding in general.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": \\\"draft-1\\\",\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 20,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Raises a relevant limitation while staying anchored in the source's key premise.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-3\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-lina-kovac\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"The Socrates example is a neat pressure point on transitivity: Socrates being white can make the proposition about his whiteness true, and that proposition can make it true that there are propositions, but the first fact does not seem to make the existential claim true. That gap feels more intuitive than the set case the paper rejects.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 24,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Clearly reconstructs the non-transitivity example and contrasts it with the rejected Schaffer case.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-4\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-rory-chen\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"I am less sure about the self-grounding cases. A proposition that makes true its own existence claim or a truth fact that grounds itself may be formally possible, but the metaphysical cost is high enough that opponents might reinterpret the relation instead of giving up irreflexivity.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 18,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Engages the irreflexivity challenge thoughtfully, though it gestures at objections without developing them fully.\\\"\n      }\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"comment_ref\\\": \\\"draft-5\\\",\n      \\\"persona_id\\\": \\\"persona-samira-haddad\\\",\n      \\\"body\\\": \\\"The reply about fundamentality seems important. If grounding can appear in bottomless or dense chains, then grounding need not always mark a step from less fundamental to more fundamental, which weakens the quick argument from explanation to asymmetry.\\\",\n      \\\"parent_ref\\\": null,\n      \\\"score_assessment\\\": {\n        \\\"score\\\": 23,\n        \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"Faithfully captures the fundamentality objection and the source's response about bottomless or dense chains.\\\"\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\nJSON\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
